00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:11,120
I my understanding right now is that you you have a team at least in some capacity.

325
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:16,960
Like walk through the process you know you're completely on your own to where you're at now.

326
00:26:17,120 --> 00:26:22,400
I'm just curious in terms of the company itself and employees or whatever they are at this stage.

327
00:26:22,880 --> 00:26:22,960
Yeah.

328
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:33,120
So I think the first big project was like actually founding a company, which is a lot more complex in Germany than I believe in like in some other countries.

329
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:39,120
I've heard Germany and like paperwork and filing are sort of legendary.

330
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:39,920
Exactly.

331
00:26:40,480 --> 00:26:46,320
So I had to like drive to like a notary service and they had to read something to me.

332
00:26:47,280 --> 00:26:51,920
That was a lot that was quite complex, but luckily I had like a tax accountant for that.

333
00:26:52,160 --> 00:26:55,200
Like I couldn't imagine like doing it on my own.

334
00:26:55,360 --> 00:27:00,400
I know some people do, but that just bores me to death like reading through.

335
00:27:01,840 --> 00:27:03,520
See, I find it scary.

336
00:27:03,760 --> 00:27:04,480
That's my biggest thing.

337
00:27:04,560 --> 00:27:09,680
It's like I'm so scared of doing something wrong and ending up in jail somehow.

338
00:27:09,840 --> 00:27:10,320
Like, yeah.

339
00:27:11,280 --> 00:27:13,440
Just by not responding to a letter or something.

340
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:14,400
Yeah, exactly.

341
00:27:15,440 --> 00:27:15,520
Yeah.

342
00:27:15,920 --> 00:27:22,240
So, but once that was done, I, like, I was very, I was always very reluctant to take the next step.

343
00:27:22,400 --> 00:27:25,760
Like, I was very reluctant to, like, go full time indie.

344
00:27:26,240 --> 00:27:35,040
Like, by the time I went full time indie, I think the app made more than, like, double or like, three times my salary I was making as a worldwide student.

345
00:27:35,360 --> 00:27:35,360
Yeah.

346
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:39,920
Like economically, it didn't make any sense to still stay at that company.

347
00:27:40,320 --> 00:27:45,680
You were not taking a leap of faith so much as finally letting go of the regular job.

348
00:27:46,720 --> 00:27:51,360
Yeah, for me, it was like a lot of the time it was like thinking about, like, what if I quit now?

349
00:27:51,520 --> 00:27:56,960
Then I like, I don't like, I work on my own, which like, sounds a bit boring.

350
00:27:57,680 --> 00:28:00,080
Also, I don't like, learn anything from colleagues.

351
00:28:00,640 --> 00:28:07,760
But the day I quit, like, I liked the job I was doing before, but like, I felt such, like, such a relief because there were no more meetings.

352
00:28:08,160 --> 00:28:15,760
And then like, I could like, if a friend would like to text me like, do you want to get a coffee, like I could always say yes, which was very nice.

353
00:28:16,400 --> 00:28:40,560
And then, of course, that's also like one of the reasons I then like it took me a long time to actually hire someone because then, of course, you can have like very few meetings, but you still like have like some like some more responsibility and you you might like have like not fixed working hours or anything, but if you like, you can't like just disappear for a month or something like that.

354
00:28:41,360 --> 00:28:48,640
Every node in the collaboration web is like a multiplier on the amount of time you have to spend.

355
00:28:49,360 --> 00:28:54,080
in meetings, in slack threads, whatever it is you want to say it is, it's still like overhead.

356
00:28:54,960 --> 00:28:55,520
Yes, yes.

357
00:28:55,680 --> 00:29:00,080
And you have to communicate more like, like also like code wise, right?

358
00:29:00,320 --> 00:29:08,560
Like everyone that like works on their own project, like some other people might be more organized than me, but my code had almost zero code comments.

359
00:29:09,040 --> 00:29:10,960
And like everything was in my head.

360
00:29:11,200 --> 00:29:15,600
Of course, that's also not good if you're doing it on your own, but that was the route I chose.

361
00:29:16,240 --> 00:29:23,040
And then as soon as people like other people start collaborating on that code, like I was like, okay, I need to like clean this up.

362
00:29:23,360 --> 00:29:26,480
I can't imagine people looking at it Dark Noise.

363
00:29:27,440 --> 00:29:31,920
I don't even like looking at it when it's, you know, a year old from stuff that I've written.

364
00:29:32,080 --> 00:29:33,760
I can't imagine letting someone else see that.

365
00:29:34,640 --> 00:29:35,440
Yes, yes.

366
00:29:37,040 --> 00:29:49,520
But the first person that started working with me was like a good friend of mine and that was for customer support because like that was a thing where like I was like I didn't enjoy doing that.

367
00:29:49,680 --> 00:29:52,640
Like I think a lot of people do enjoy doing it.

368
00:29:52,720 --> 00:29:56,240
A lot of indies like getting like emails, like feedback.

369
00:29:56,480 --> 00:29:59,840
Most people are super nice, responding to them.

370
00:30:00,480 --> 00:30:09,120
But at that point, it was like a volume, like a level where I would like to spend like around like two hours a day doing it.

371
00:30:09,280 --> 00:30:09,360
Yeah.

372
00:30:10,320 --> 00:30:18,800
And especially like I think like the nice thing about being in India is you can like take like a week off or something like that spontaneously.

373
00:30:19,840 --> 00:30:23,760
And then of course like stuff doesn't go like your app doesn't move forward.

374
00:30:24,400 --> 00:30:27,200
But usually things don't like really pile up.

375
00:30:27,840 --> 00:30:29,360
But those emails stay pileed up.

376
00:30:29,520 --> 00:30:37,440
So if I didn't do it for like a longer weekend, then I would come to like 100, 120 emails, which takes you like hours on end.

377
00:30:37,680 --> 00:30:37,760
Yeah.

378
00:30:38,160 --> 00:30:43,440
Like doing like the same, like answering the same thing, uh, replying again and again.

379
00:30:43,680 --> 00:30:45,600
Yeah, you have to do this, you have to do that.

380
00:30:46,400 --> 00:30:48,080
And that was like super tiring.

381
00:30:48,400 --> 00:30:49,440
Uh, and I'm not the person.

382
00:30:49,680 --> 00:30:51,040
I'm not the right person to do that.

383
00:30:51,360 --> 00:30:56,720
So, uh, like a friend of mine, he was like already like working in that field.

384
00:30:56,880 --> 00:31:02,800
And he was also super interested in like doing the social media for structured, which I also don't really enjoy.

385
00:31:03,120 --> 00:31:05,280
Like, I'm not a social media guy.

386
00:31:05,920 --> 00:31:09,360
So, he basically started as a freelancer doing that.

387
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:16,480
And that was super helpful because then I could focus like on building, like building the app and not like answering emails.

388
00:31:17,040 --> 00:31:27,120
I noticed something like looking around your website, you you also have a nice like help page that has kind of questions that frequently ask questions with answers to them or whatever.

389
00:31:27,520 --> 00:31:30,960
But I noticed you're using something that I somehow had never come across called Helpkit.

390
00:31:31,680 --> 00:31:31,760
Yes.

391
00:31:32,320 --> 00:31:41,600
That as far as I can tell, it's like letting you do all your support docs in Notion and then it just automatically publishes it to your website.

392
00:31:42,480 --> 00:31:42,560
Yes.

393
00:31:42,880 --> 00:31:43,600
Yes, exactly.

394
00:31:43,840 --> 00:31:48,640
So that that is super helpful because like, I use Notion anyways and you just.

395
00:31:49,360 --> 00:31:50,000
put everything there.

396
00:31:50,240 --> 00:31:57,840
Notion has a pretty good editor, like with like support for like not markdown, but like markdown style editing.

397
00:31:58,000 --> 00:31:58,160
Right.

398
00:31:58,320 --> 00:32:02,480
And like putting images in there, stuff like that, like organizing the stuff a certain way.

399
00:32:03,120 --> 00:32:10,240
And that service, also built by an indie, just like takes that Notion page and parses it in a certain way.

400
00:32:10,560 --> 00:32:15,840
And then it like transforms that into a help center with like search and everything.

401
00:32:16,160 --> 00:32:17,840
You can like configure tons of stuff.

402
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:21,280
You actually like have like feedback integrated into it.

403
00:32:21,520 --> 00:32:24,880
So I can like see people can like leave feedback at the bottom of the page.

404
00:32:24,880 --> 00:32:26,240
At the bottom of the page, I can see that feedback.

405
00:32:26,960 --> 00:32:32,880
So that's super helpful because, of course, like every email that is not sent is better for us and the user as well.

406
00:32:33,200 --> 00:32:41,600
Did you find adding stuff like that has helped reduce the load a little bit or is the load growing at such a rate that you wouldn't even notice?

407
00:32:42,400 --> 00:32:49,680
Yeah, it's hard to say because we like at first it was like super minimal the help center and like we gradually added more stuff.

408
00:32:50,880 --> 00:32:55,520
So it's hard to say if it's like if it has reduced the number of emails.

409
00:32:57,520 --> 00:33:02,400
We certainly still get emails from people that obviously didn't read the help center.

410
00:33:02,720 --> 00:33:02,800
Yeah.

411
00:33:03,200 --> 00:33:07,760
Even though within the app, you don't find a button to contact us.

412
00:33:08,160 --> 00:33:10,000
You find a button that opens the help center.

413
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:12,240
And then at the bottom of the help center, you find a button to contact.

414
00:33:12,800 --> 00:33:15,760
They have to go through the help center before they do.

415
00:33:16,080 --> 00:33:28,000
They'd rather like write an email than use a search box, but I think that's especially for people that are not that tech savvy or are not that introverted, they like writing emails.

416
00:33:28,320 --> 00:33:28,400
Yeah.

417
00:33:29,280 --> 00:33:29,360
Yeah.

418
00:33:29,600 --> 00:33:33,680
And I mean, you know, what they're looking for might not necessarily be easy to find or whatever.

419
00:33:33,920 --> 00:33:36,160
They basically use different terms, right?

420
00:33:36,320 --> 00:33:36,400
So.

