00:33:36,560 --> 00:33:46,960
So that's like the hard part of customer support, like translating the terms that people use outside the company into terms that we use, uh, inside the company.

422
00:33:47,200 --> 00:33:55,360
And, uh, of course, like the people that just, like, discovered the app like two minutes ago, they don't know what we call like a recurring task or something.

423
00:33:55,920 --> 00:33:56,240
Exactly.

424
00:33:56,720 --> 00:33:56,800
Yeah.

425
00:33:57,120 --> 00:34:02,640
Do you how do you go about, like, adding to and updating those, those docs?

426
00:34:03,040 --> 00:34:24,320
Like, I remember talking to Sean Hickman, he gave me the idea, which I think is probably what a lot of people do of like, every time you get a support email, look at that as an opportunity for like, how could you have, how could you have sent that person through the helpdesk funnel instead of ending up in your inbox and either add a dock or tweak your dock with new keywords or whatever to pick up their terms?

427
00:34:25,520 --> 00:34:25,600
Yeah.

428
00:34:26,080 --> 00:34:26,160
Yeah.

429
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:34,720
We we do that like on a like on a quite regular basis, like look at what, what are people, what are people coming to us with.

430
00:34:35,040 --> 00:34:48,720
Like a lot of the time, the email actually involves just like sending them a link to a help center article, like just because like, of course, it's much better to like point them to like a fully written out help center article than like sending them like a three line email.

431
00:34:49,360 --> 00:34:50,000
explaining the thing.

432
00:34:50,720 --> 00:34:54,560
Um, but then also we updated from time to time.

433
00:34:55,200 --> 00:35:01,360
Uh, what is super nice about help good again is you get, uh, what people have searched for but did not find an answer to.

434
00:35:01,920 --> 00:35:03,120
Uh, so that's interesting.

